==English==
{{wp|Realia (library science)}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|LL.|realia}}, neuter plural of {{m|la|realis||real}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹeɪˈɑːlɪə/|/ɹiːˈeɪlɪə/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-realia.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑːliə|eɪliə|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|p}}

# Objects from real life or from the real world, as opposed to [[theoretical]] [[constructs]] or [[fabricated]] examples.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=w:Norman Davies|title=Vanished Kingdoms|page=28|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012
|passage=It might be possible, for example, to work backwards from the known '''realia''' of Visigothic Spain.}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} Words and expressions for culture-specific material elements.
# {{lb|en|[[library|libraries]]|information science}} [[physical|Physical]] objects in a [[library]] [[collection]] that do not fit into traditional categories of [[media]], whose media are the objects themselves, as opposed to their [[informational]] content, such as [[artifact|artifacts]], [[tool|tools]], [[memorabilia]], and naturally-occurring [[specimen|specimens]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2002-04-14|title=Catalog This|journal=w:The New York Times|author=w:Lev Grossman|section=Education Life|page=26|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2002/04/14/education/catalog-this.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210512021045/https://www.nytimes.com/2002/04/14/education/catalog-this.html|archivedate=2021-05-21|passage="We acquire entire collections of materials for research purposes," explains Saundra Taylor, curator of manuscripts at the Lilly Library at Indiana University in Bloomington. "That inevitably includes something that's not books or manuscripts."{{pb}}Such as? "We have teeth, hair, all kinds of goofy things like that," says Katharine Salzmann, archivist and manuscripts curator at Southern Illinois University in Carbondale. "We don't set out to buy them. It's just a happenstance kind of thing. Someone tosses them in the box, or forgets they're in the envelope."{{pb}}These unanticipated acquisitions are referred to in the trade variously as personal effects, ephemera, artifacts, memorabilia and, perhaps most evocatively, '''realia'''.}}

